  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "eb2cd88f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "At a high level, all baseline models show limited predictive power although there are key disticntions to highlight among them. \n",
    "Linear SVM was the model with the top F1 scores, followed by Logistic Regression and lastly XGBoost. \n",
    "Overall performance remained limited with the highes F1Score being 0.56 from the linear SVM model containing metadata and full bill text.   "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 21,
   "id": "ec009df8",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1100x500 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "ch.plot_model_comparison(baseline_results)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c3ea8d7f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "From the chart above we can see Linear SVM validation macro F1 score higher across all features sets compared to the other two models. A key finding is that Linear SVM was the only model to show incremental performance as text features were introduced with its validation f1 increasing from approximately .53 using metadata alone to .56 whe we add full bill text. Although this may be a small improvement, it suggests that Linear SVM is better able to leverage textual information. This may not dictate the final model selection after finetunning but it points to Linear SVM as the top candidate."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 22,
   "id": "d961c5a1",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1100x500 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "ch.plot_generalization_gap(baseline_results)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b28f4d0c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "In this chart we can see the gap from Training and Validation F1 scores. XGBoost had the highest differences with bill_text being almost half a point, and appears to have been incrementing the gap as new text features were introduced. This reflects that XGboost tends to overfit and is not able to generalize as bill text is introduced. We may want to consider not pursuing XGBoosting during finetuning. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 23,
   "id": "3c773807",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1100x500 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "ch.plot_passed_recall(baseline_results)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e57609ed",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Lastly, recall for the Passed class remains low across all models, with Linear SVM using full legislative text achieving the highest recall of approximately 0.238 (23.8%). Although this represents relatively limited performance in identifying passed legislation, it shows Linear SVM's ability to outperform Logistic Regression and XGBoost on the class of greatest interest.\n",
    "\n",
    "Nevertheless, at the baseline our strongest model fails to correctly identify approximately 76% of passed bills, which points a great need of improvement from these baselines. These findings reinforce the need to address class imbalance, potential data leakage, and model generalization in future analysis. "
   ]
  },
